  "final_text": "The dashboard and the alert rule both exist, and the rule is enabled. I haven't opened the dashboard in Kibana or checked that the rule has run.\n\n**Data:** The \"Kibana Sample Data Logs\" data view covers `kibana_sample_data_logs`. Its key fields are:\n- `timestamp`\n- `bytes`\n- `clientip`\n- `response` (text, with a `response.keyword` subfield)\n- `extension`\n- `geo.src` and `geo.dest`\n- `machine.os`\n- `url`\n- `agent`\n\n**Dashboard:** \"EFF-fa55bf1f Web logs overview\" (id `b59f58b0-7f15-4958-8a4f-6e512a56b1c6`) has five panels:\n- Total requests, shown as a metric.\n- Average bytes, shown as a metric.\n- Unique clients, shown as a metric.\n- Requests over time, shown as a line chart.\n- Top response codes, shown as a table.\n\nMy first attempt used ES|QL for the two charts and Kibana rejected it, so both use the data view instead. Only one dashboard with this title exists, so the command that hung earlier didn't create a duplicate.\n\n**Rule:** \"EFF-fa55bf1f 5xx spike\" (id `239f30c0-5d0b-4451-bae3-c76f36b31522`) is an Elasticsearch query rule.\n- **Schedule:** it runs every minute.\n- **Match:** it counts documents in `kibana_sample_data_logs` where `response.keyword` matches `5[0-9][0-9]`.\n- **Condition:** it fires when that count is above 10 over a 5-minute window.\n- **Actions:** it has none, so a firing only shows up in Kibana's alerts, not as a notification.",
